 at the end, `~a`) are rejected with the exact error message; `~0` and `~1` are decoded in the right order (`/~01` means `~1`, not `/`).\n- **Escaping:** `escape` and `unescape` results, and that one undoes the other.\n- **List indexes:** rejected forms like `01`, `00`, `-1`, `+1`, ` 1`, `1.0`, `1a` and empty; multi-digit indexes like `/10` work.\n- **Error messages:** out-of-bounds indexes, missing keys (with `VERBOSE_EXCEPTIONS` both on and off), strings, and values that can't be indexed. The `default` argument is checked to suppress errors and return the fallback.\n- **The `-` token:** at the end of a list it returns a marker object (checked by its `repr`); in a dict it is an ordinary key.\n- **Other indexable objects:** custom classes that support `[]` lookups.\n- **`set`:** both in-place and on a copy (the original stays unchanged), setting the root, appending with `-`, and the errors for strings, out-of-range indexes and tuples.\n- **Pointer operations:** `contains` and `in`, joining with `join` and `/` (including subclasses and invalid suffixes), `path`, `str` and `repr`, `from_parts`, and equality and hashing.\n- **`pairwise`:** empty, single-item, list and iterator inputs.\n\n**Mutation check:** I ran a quick mutation script of my own (mutmut isn't installed here). It made 106 mutants on a temporary copy of the library. The tests caught 86 of them. Of the 20 that survived:\n- 19 only changed docstrings or doctest text, so no test can detect them.\n- The other one deletes the `raise` on the \"Invalid sequence index\" path. That line can't be reached: the earlier regex check only lets plain ASCII digits through, and `int()` always succeeds on those. So that change doesn't alter how the library behaves.\n\nSo every mutant that actually changed behaviour was caught. This was a smaller, simpler set of changes than a full mutation tool would make, so the real score could be somewhat lower.","ttft_ms":2743,"type":"result","duration_ms":114590,"uuid":"47c9ea1f-f4ac